WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.269 --> 00:00:02.750
It is great to welcome back to the program
today.

00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:09.590
Dr. Christopher DeCarlo, who's an award winning
philosopher, educator and author of the latest

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:14.879
book on critical thinking called So You Think
You Can Think Tools for Having Intelligent

00:00:14.879 --> 00:00:17.180
Conversations and Getting Along.

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:19.460
It's great to have you back on.

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:20.460
Good to be here.

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:21.460
Always a pleasure.

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:28.130
So a common question I get from viewers when
I take live calls is I have family members

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:30.690
who believe this or who believe that.

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:34.520
And I want to know how it is that I should
confront them.

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:39.329
And usually my answer is that depends on how
willing you are to ruin the relationship.

00:00:39.329 --> 00:00:46.170
In other words, there's ways to do this more
lightly with a Socratic method of questioning

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:49.750
and sort of gentle inquiry into how one formed
their beliefs.

00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:55.370
There's ways to have these conversations much
more pointedly and aggressively.

00:00:55.370 --> 00:01:02.590
Give me your sort of 30000 foot view on how
some of these often difficult conversations

00:01:02.590 --> 00:01:04.019
can be approached.

00:01:04.019 --> 00:01:05.019
Right.

00:01:05.019 --> 00:01:09.060
So in this book, I talk about spectral discussions.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:15.109
And what I mean by that is if an issue can
be polarized and many of them are polarized,

00:01:15.109 --> 00:01:23.180
I take, for example, gun control, something
to which your country deals with on a daily

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:24.869
basis.

00:01:24.869 --> 00:01:28.179
You know, what you do is you look at the extremes
of that.

00:01:28.179 --> 00:01:29.189
What what should we do?

00:01:29.189 --> 00:01:31.759
Well, allow all guns everywhere, no matter
what.

00:01:31.759 --> 00:01:33.139
You can drive a tank to work.

00:01:33.139 --> 00:01:35.349
You know, you can do all of that kind of stuff.

00:01:35.349 --> 00:01:37.560
The other end of the spectrum, no guns whatsoever.

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:39.749
You know, all that kind of thing.

00:01:39.749 --> 00:01:46.990
And then start by determining where on the
spectrum the two discussants lie, like, where

00:01:46.990 --> 00:01:48.439
are you?

00:01:48.439 --> 00:01:52.279
And then see how how polarized those views
are.

00:01:52.279 --> 00:01:58.451
And then try to imagine, is there any common
area between those two positions and what

00:01:58.451 --> 00:01:59.451
are those?

00:01:59.451 --> 00:02:02.590
So, you know, start with what is your argument?

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:04.380
What is it that you believe?

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:06.679
Allow me to understand that.

00:02:06.679 --> 00:02:11.530
Have each other say what their arguments are
back to each other.

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:12.640
We call it Steel Manning.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:14.110
You're probably familiar with this.

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:15.110
Yeah.

00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:19.220
Rather than Straw manning the other person's
argument and making a mockery.

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:20.530
You really want to say.

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:21.530
OK.

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:22.530
So let me get this straight.

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:23.700
Is this what you believe?

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:26.420
And then you lay it on the line, then they
can do the same.

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:29.520
Now you know where you are on that spectrum.

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:33.200
And now you can talk about, you know, would
we be able to change our views?

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:37.860
Is there any evidence or any information that
might draws closer together?

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:40.940
So I'm finding that this method is becoming
very helpful.

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:41.940
Yes.

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:43.540
Peter Bogosian has described that.

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:46.430
What would cause you to change your mind if
it were the case?

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:52.460
The feasibility tests to see if someone is
even open to to reexamining their their beliefs.

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:58.650
Now, when it comes to I mean, to pick something
that has come up recently here in the United

00:02:58.650 --> 00:02:59.650
States.

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:02.340
And by the way, so Christopher's Canadian
and that's why he said your country.

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:07.080
And just so people understand the context
here of the discussion here in the United

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:08.080
States.

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:14.800
There's a big controversy now around statues,
Confederate era statues and statues reminiscent

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:15.800
of slavery.

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:21.250
And one of the critiques I had of a lot of
the conversations that are happening are that

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:28.190
the conversations in media are often only
represented by extreme views, meaning you

00:03:28.190 --> 00:03:31.000
should never take a statue down.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:36.630
And on the other hand, take down George Washington
because he owns like these extremes, whereas

00:03:36.630 --> 00:03:42.960
most people probably would be able to have
a more nuanced conversation, like, well, listen,

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:44.910
there was an era where everybody had slaves.

00:03:44.910 --> 00:03:51.650
And what we should look at is maybe more whether
the individual is known for promoting slavery

00:03:51.650 --> 00:03:53.740
versus whether they're really right.

00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:58.680
So that that seems to be a big part of the
conversation, that often the conversations

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:00.930
are only happening in extremes.

00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:04.319
Yeah, and it's unfortunate, right.

00:04:04.319 --> 00:04:08.319
That and the media is partially to blame because
they love the extremes.

00:04:08.319 --> 00:04:09.319
Right.

00:04:09.319 --> 00:04:10.319
You've got a great story.

00:04:10.319 --> 00:04:13.370
You get more clicks, you get more hits, you
get more viewers, whatever it is.

00:04:13.370 --> 00:04:19.150
You know, we we were fascinated with the confrontation,
as it were.

00:04:19.150 --> 00:04:21.609
We've lost the capacity to appreciate nuance.

00:04:21.609 --> 00:04:28.419
And it looks like we're in a baby out with
the bathwater kind of movement right now where,

00:04:28.419 --> 00:04:33.569
you know, no matter what good a person has
done in their life, if during those times

00:04:33.569 --> 00:04:38.680
they were involved in other types of activities
which are now deemed quite reprehensible or

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:44.110
horrific or horrible or whatever it is, then
everything that that person might have accomplished

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:50.800
that was good is ignored because they were
involved with these other kind of nefarious

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:52.979
or or questionable.

00:04:52.979 --> 00:04:57.930
Types of moral activities, and we need to
have the conversation around these statues

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:02.240
to look at them and say, you know what good,
if any, has.

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:07.080
Has this person done for humanity and what
are the bad things and use it to use these

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:08.529
as teachable moments.

00:05:08.529 --> 00:05:12.610
And if if they if they are indeed offensive,
for whatever reasons, should we just put them

00:05:12.610 --> 00:05:18.310
in museums then and then use them as for educational
purposes to say, yes, this person did X, Y

00:05:18.310 --> 00:05:19.310
and Z.

00:05:19.310 --> 00:05:20.310
But you know what?

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:22.080
They also did this as well.

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:25.180
You know, I teach at Ryerson University.

00:05:25.180 --> 00:05:30.779
Edgerton Ryerson was responsible for the the
school systems that put a lot of Aboriginal

00:05:30.779 --> 00:05:34.999
kids, took them away from their parents and
put them in these schools what what's called

00:05:34.999 --> 00:05:37.249
the residential school problem.

00:05:37.249 --> 00:05:40.120
But his statue is still standing at Ryerson
University.

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:44.620
I'm wondering if it's just a matter of time
before, you know, people think, you know,

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:46.379
that's got to come down as well.

00:05:46.379 --> 00:05:51.759
So he did a lot of great things for education,
but he also did some not so great things.

00:05:51.759 --> 00:05:57.499
It's the world is more complicated if if we
hold our heroes, you know, those in high esteem

00:05:57.499 --> 00:06:02.409
too high, they have that much further to fall
when we realize nobody's perfect.

00:06:02.409 --> 00:06:09.969
And, you know, we are a new monts, difficult
species at best here in the United States.

00:06:09.969 --> 00:06:14.039
We are experiencing the deadly repercussions.

00:06:14.039 --> 00:06:20.449
I don't think that's an exaggeration of a
critical thinking gap or lack when it comes

00:06:20.449 --> 00:06:21.949
to coronavirus.

00:06:21.949 --> 00:06:25.680
And this applies to understanding how masks
work.

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:30.720
This comes to ideas like if we tested less,
we'd have fewer cases.

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:34.610
I mean, there's no shortage of sort of examples.

00:06:34.610 --> 00:06:39.580
What do you make of the way in which this
is failing in the United States?

00:06:39.580 --> 00:06:40.990
Is it education?

00:06:40.990 --> 00:06:42.070
Is it culture?

00:06:42.070 --> 00:06:43.070
Is it's economics?

00:06:43.070 --> 00:06:45.350
I mean, like, where do we start?

00:06:45.350 --> 00:06:46.350
Yeah.

00:06:46.350 --> 00:06:48.849
There are so many systems involved here.

00:06:48.849 --> 00:06:49.849
Right.

00:06:49.849 --> 00:06:53.960
And some of the work I'm doing in critical
thinking is something I call relational systemic,

00:06:53.960 --> 00:06:59.659
which is to look at all the systems that are
involved that interplay and cause people to

00:06:59.659 --> 00:07:02.339
think the way they do at a given time in history.

00:07:02.339 --> 00:07:05.530
Yeah, we can look at the education systems
for sure.

00:07:05.530 --> 00:07:09.120
But you've got to understand, you know, Meems
can kill you.

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:13.259
I mean, I've written about, you know, memetic
equilibrium and the idea that when you get

00:07:13.259 --> 00:07:18.879
comfortable with a set of ideas, it doesn't
matter if they run counter to reality.

00:07:18.879 --> 00:07:22.330
You know, if ideology can outweigh reality.

00:07:22.330 --> 00:07:25.539
And in so doing, you mentioned that weaponized
ignorance.

00:07:25.539 --> 00:07:30.729
You you talk about the idea that the mask
has become a symbol of oppression in your

00:07:30.729 --> 00:07:32.360
country for some people.

00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:37.839
And that is absolutely bizarre for the rest
of the world to look at that and think you're

00:07:37.839 --> 00:07:40.729
missing a really important point here.

00:07:40.729 --> 00:07:43.550
And that is this is a public health issue.

00:07:43.550 --> 00:07:44.729
It's not as it's apolitical.

00:07:44.729 --> 00:07:47.370
It has nothing to do with politics.

00:07:47.370 --> 00:07:48.720
We want everybody to survive.

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:50.580
We don't care what your politics is.

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:51.809
We don't want you to die.

00:07:51.809 --> 00:07:56.949
We don't want you to feel discomfort or to
spread the disease any further.

00:07:56.949 --> 00:08:04.559
So, you know, that is a horrific example of
weaponized ignorance and using it as a tool

00:08:04.559 --> 00:08:07.629
or a symbolic representation of a political
movement.

00:08:07.629 --> 00:08:14.080
So some people in my audience feel very strongly,
and I've even said this before, that if critical

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:18.599
thinking and epistemology and philosophy were
a part of everybody's schooling, starting

00:08:18.599 --> 00:08:23.210
probably around seventh grade, age 12, that
a lot of this that we're seeing would be short

00:08:23.210 --> 00:08:24.830
circuited and preempted altogether.

00:08:24.830 --> 00:08:30.389
But then I do get pushback from people who
maybe people who work in school systems that

00:08:30.389 --> 00:08:34.660
are teaching these things to some degree,
saying the problem is bigger than that.

00:08:34.660 --> 00:08:37.440
And you have to understand that teachers can
only do so much anyway.

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:40.410
And a lot of it is what's going on at home
and what's going on in culture and what's

00:08:40.410 --> 00:08:41.729
going on in the media.

00:08:41.729 --> 00:08:43.700
So what's the lowest hanging fruit?

00:08:43.700 --> 00:08:45.130
Where do we start to turn this around?

00:08:45.130 --> 00:08:48.850
And what could have the best the biggest effect
most quickly?

00:08:48.850 --> 00:08:49.850
Right.

00:08:49.850 --> 00:08:54.430
I I've been lobbying governments here in Canada
and have had some success with trying to get

00:08:54.430 --> 00:08:59.310
critical thinking taught at first of all at
the high school system.

00:08:59.310 --> 00:09:01.090
You know very well, David.

00:09:01.090 --> 00:09:04.530
That critical thinking teaches people how
to think right.

00:09:04.530 --> 00:09:11.040
It's the tools that allow them to better understand
information and then to determine the acceptability

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:14.860
of that information and whether or not they
should act on that information.

00:09:14.860 --> 00:09:20.590
And I do very much believe if we can get critical
thinking into the schools as early as possible,

00:09:20.590 --> 00:09:25.460
I believe seven and eight is probably the
right time to start to introduce it, to get

00:09:25.460 --> 00:09:26.460
it going.

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:32.530
I think we'd be much more difficult for people
to then say, I don't care what science says.

00:09:32.530 --> 00:09:38.230
You know, my leader maintains X. Yes, he cuts
it empowers people.

00:09:38.230 --> 00:09:41.770
Critical thinking isn't just something you
do.

00:09:41.770 --> 00:09:43.600
It's what you live.

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:49.110
And we have to make that a part of people's
lives so that it becomes almost reflexive.

00:09:49.110 --> 00:09:56.190
It almost becomes a reflex action when information
is is presented in a particular way that is

00:09:56.190 --> 00:10:00.430
false to the way in which the natural world
actually functions.

00:10:00.430 --> 00:10:01.540
And that's where we need to start.

00:10:01.540 --> 00:10:07.900
We need to start at those those middle age
grades, seven and eight and all the way through

00:10:07.900 --> 00:10:09.660
high school for sure.

00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:15.060
From from the standpoint of the virus and
what you've observed, what if we do a sort

00:10:15.060 --> 00:10:20.550
of analysis, analysis of fallacious thinking
or fallacious assertions?

00:10:20.550 --> 00:10:25.700
What are the main fallacies that you're seeing
in the context of discussions about cases,

00:10:25.700 --> 00:10:28.670
viral spread, contagion, treatment masks,
et cetera?

00:10:28.670 --> 00:10:32.760
I mean, just get you know, if you have a couple
bullet points on that, right?

00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:37.950
Well, first of all, it's the it's the misinformation
and disinformation we have to stop.

00:10:37.950 --> 00:10:43.770
And that's, you know, at what was a twains
that a lie can make its self around the world,

00:10:43.770 --> 00:10:47.270
you know, halfway around the world while the
truth is putting its shoes on or whatever.

00:10:47.270 --> 00:10:53.280
So once ments misinformation gets out there,
we need to focus on how to combat that quickly

00:10:53.280 --> 00:10:55.070
and effectively.

00:10:55.070 --> 00:10:58.110
And to me, there's no easy solution for that.

00:10:58.110 --> 00:11:04.741
Social media has gotten to the point where
influencers are being trusted more than, you

00:11:04.741 --> 00:11:06.490
know, scientists.

00:11:06.490 --> 00:11:07.490
Yes.

00:11:07.490 --> 00:11:08.490
And right.

00:11:08.490 --> 00:11:17.480
Once we get to to that level, then I think
it it it becomes paramount that science and

00:11:17.480 --> 00:11:23.830
the influencers, if possible, the scientists
and the influencers could somehow work together.

00:11:23.830 --> 00:11:24.830
Right.

00:11:24.830 --> 00:11:30.780
What's horrifying about that is some attempts
were made like, for example, Dr. Foushee did

00:11:30.780 --> 00:11:37.150
a bunch of live streams with folks like Stephan
Curry, the NBA basketball player, and trying

00:11:37.150 --> 00:11:38.630
to sort of break it.

00:11:38.630 --> 00:11:43.310
It's just a I don't know if it was too little
or too late, but you've got, you know, a political

00:11:43.310 --> 00:11:48.710
movement which is listening more to a game
show host than an infectious disease doctor.

00:11:48.710 --> 00:11:50.330
I know.

00:11:50.330 --> 00:11:52.160
I just don't know.

00:11:52.160 --> 00:11:56.620
It's it just feels like we're living in an
alien's thought experiment.

00:11:56.620 --> 00:11:58.430
It just seems so weird.

00:11:58.430 --> 00:11:59.750
What what what's happening.

00:11:59.750 --> 00:12:00.750
Right.

00:12:00.750 --> 00:12:05.260
To to see this occur and to know what the
truth is.

00:12:05.260 --> 00:12:06.260
And I'm not talking.

00:12:06.260 --> 00:12:08.700
I'm a big tree, big t metaphysical truth or
anything.

00:12:08.700 --> 00:12:11.930
I'm just talking about scientific probabilistic
truth.

00:12:11.930 --> 00:12:15.120
That is for the benefit of all humankind.

00:12:15.120 --> 00:12:21.790
And I really I'm at a loss with this one in
terms of what's the best way to engage in

00:12:21.790 --> 00:12:27.800
having the conversation, like how much evidence
can be provided in order to convince somebody

00:12:27.800 --> 00:12:30.240
that they really ought to wear a mask.

00:12:30.240 --> 00:12:31.240
Right.

00:12:31.240 --> 00:12:35.690
That it's not a political statement that,
you know, finally Trump wore one for the first

00:12:35.690 --> 00:12:36.690
time.

00:12:36.690 --> 00:12:40.060
I think, you know, the other day, what was
he at Walter Reed or wherever?

00:12:40.060 --> 00:12:41.060
Yeah.

00:12:41.060 --> 00:12:43.530
You know, it was they basically said you're
wearing one.

00:12:43.530 --> 00:12:44.810
So that that's good.

00:12:44.810 --> 00:12:46.610
Somebody's got to stand up to this guy.

00:12:46.610 --> 00:12:51.390
Like, I don't know why people aren't, but
maybe that's just the cult of personality

00:12:51.390 --> 00:12:52.390
that he has.

00:12:52.390 --> 00:12:53.390
All right.

00:12:53.390 --> 00:12:58.890
When it comes to statistics, it's of course,
we know very much about how statistics can

00:12:58.890 --> 00:13:02.810
be shaped in order to give a the impression
of A or the opposite of a.

00:13:02.810 --> 00:13:06.660
And a lot of it is just understanding the
mechanisms that are used.

00:13:06.660 --> 00:13:12.220
What specifically can you recommend to the
audience in order to parse statistics for

00:13:12.220 --> 00:13:17.360
themselves, like when Donald Trump says we're
testing more than anyone or the death rate

00:13:17.360 --> 00:13:19.470
has to come down tenfold?

00:13:19.470 --> 00:13:22.590
Is this a mathematical understanding that
people need?

00:13:22.590 --> 00:13:25.110
Or is it more of a critical thinking, understanding?

00:13:25.110 --> 00:13:26.110
Both.

00:13:26.110 --> 00:13:31.380
Like I really do think statistical and probabilistic
reasoning is a part of critical thinking,

00:13:31.380 --> 00:13:34.880
and it should be taught fairly early on.

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:38.800
And I'm actually dealing with teachers right
now who have who are, you know, creatively

00:13:38.800 --> 00:13:43.730
using the pandemic to teach students about
statistics and probability.

00:13:43.730 --> 00:13:50.340
It's a wonderful teachable moment that they're
using under such hard conditions.

00:13:50.340 --> 00:13:55.340
It's you know, it's like, you know, the flower
emerging out of, you know, the manure kind

00:13:55.340 --> 00:13:56.340
of thing.

00:13:56.340 --> 00:13:57.640
It's wonderful to see them take.

00:13:57.640 --> 00:14:03.470
This as a teachable moment that they can then
say, look, you know, as I say, this is a numbers

00:14:03.470 --> 00:14:04.470
game.

00:14:04.470 --> 00:14:05.470
This comes down to numbers.

00:14:05.470 --> 00:14:09.980
And the better numbers we have, the more accurate
we can be with those numbers.

00:14:09.980 --> 00:14:13.040
Testing, tracing, tracking.

00:14:13.040 --> 00:14:14.040
Knowing who has it.

00:14:14.040 --> 00:14:15.360
Knowing who doesn't have it.

00:14:15.360 --> 00:14:17.570
Look, until there's a vaccine.

00:14:17.570 --> 00:14:18.960
This is what we've got.

00:14:18.960 --> 00:14:26.390
This is our defense, especially with a virus
that has, you know, such a large, asymptomatic

00:14:26.390 --> 00:14:27.390
population.

00:14:27.390 --> 00:14:29.240
And that's really what we're dealing with
here.

00:14:29.240 --> 00:14:31.390
And nobody really wants to address that.

00:14:31.390 --> 00:14:37.120
But the fact of the matter is we can solve
a lot of the issues if it's done intelligently

00:14:37.120 --> 00:14:39.870
and if we have the right numbers.

00:14:39.870 --> 00:14:42.930
But we have to be together on this in the
United States.

00:14:42.930 --> 00:14:49.770
If you look at county data, you see that the
growth rates of cases are significantly higher

00:14:49.770 --> 00:14:53.930
in counties that voted for Donald Trump relative
to counties that voted for Hillary Clinton.

00:14:53.930 --> 00:14:59.160
But if we step back from from that, maybe
so specifically in your work, have there been

00:14:59.160 --> 00:15:05.480
certain groups that seem to do better when
it comes to critical thinking than worse?

00:15:05.480 --> 00:15:09.670
And I'm not talking about genetically or anything
like that, but through cultural and education

00:15:09.670 --> 00:15:14.370
reasons, whether it's Democrats versus Republicans
or religious versus non-religious or whatever

00:15:14.370 --> 00:15:15.370
the case may be.

00:15:15.370 --> 00:15:17.880
Are there trends you've observed?

00:15:17.880 --> 00:15:19.070
That's a great question.

00:15:19.070 --> 00:15:26.740
You know, I mean, we we have seen that those
who are more fervently religious have a little

00:15:26.740 --> 00:15:31.980
bit more difficult time reconciling inconsistencies
with their world views.

00:15:31.980 --> 00:15:33.110
But that's to be expected.

00:15:33.110 --> 00:15:34.110
Right.

00:15:34.110 --> 00:15:39.310
You know, my my take on religion is basically,
you know, you can believe in anything you

00:15:39.310 --> 00:15:40.310
want.

00:15:40.310 --> 00:15:45.260
You just can't harm people through those beliefs
or harm other species through those beliefs.

00:15:45.260 --> 00:15:50.890
So if you're so dogmatic, you've reached a
point where you refuse to entertain the possibility

00:15:50.890 --> 00:15:52.600
that you could be wrong.

00:15:52.600 --> 00:15:54.720
You see the obvious danger in that.

00:15:54.720 --> 00:15:59.840
And there's when I look at someone like Donald
Trump, there is nothing more dangerous than

00:15:59.840 --> 00:16:02.520
blind ignorance, which is not even knowing.

00:16:02.520 --> 00:16:08.150
You don't know like that's a really dangerous
epistemic state of mind to be in.

00:16:08.150 --> 00:16:09.790
And I see it continuously.

00:16:09.790 --> 00:16:16.940
And so, yeah, if a person is overly dogmatic,
that's obviously going to present barriers.

00:16:16.940 --> 00:16:17.940
Right.

00:16:17.940 --> 00:16:21.710
And then you have to adjust your strategy
for having a conversation with that person.

00:16:21.710 --> 00:16:26.970
You have to be much more Socratic, much more
gentle in your approach, because the harder

00:16:26.970 --> 00:16:32.450
you try to consider or analyze their beliefs,
the more they're going to dig in and say,

00:16:32.450 --> 00:16:35.150
you're the problem, not me.

00:16:35.150 --> 00:16:39.980
What do you think will be the long term effect
on society of of the virus?

00:16:39.980 --> 00:16:46.990
I had a conversation with Brian Greene not
long ago, and there's sometimes this instinct

00:16:46.990 --> 00:16:50.360
that this is really going to change things,
period.

00:16:50.360 --> 00:16:53.100
And public health is going to be done differently.

00:16:53.100 --> 00:16:55.920
The way people greet each other is going to
change permanently.

00:16:55.920 --> 00:17:01.640
I tend to go in the direction of things, sort
of return to the mean, and people go back

00:17:01.640 --> 00:17:02.640
to normal.

00:17:02.640 --> 00:17:08.240
And and it's very hard to get really long
lasting change for for humans.

00:17:08.240 --> 00:17:10.309
Where are you on that issue?

00:17:10.309 --> 00:17:11.309
I'm hopeful.

00:17:11.309 --> 00:17:14.640
You know, I'm cautiously optimistic.

00:17:14.640 --> 00:17:16.020
But let's face it, David.

00:17:16.020 --> 00:17:21.880
My colleagues and I have been writing to our
politicians and to politicians in China for

00:17:21.880 --> 00:17:28.260
over two decades, basically saying, look,
there's going to be a pandemic if we don't

00:17:28.260 --> 00:17:35.500
put initiatives in place that monitor the
sale of certain types of species of animals.

00:17:35.500 --> 00:17:37.550
It's just it was just a ticking time bomb.

00:17:37.550 --> 00:17:40.260
It was not a matter of if, but when.

00:17:40.260 --> 00:17:43.500
And, you know, a lot of them would come back
and say, well, we caught Serzh.

00:17:43.500 --> 00:17:47.760
You know, we took care of Meurs, we got H1
and we've got a vaccine for H1N1.

00:17:47.760 --> 00:17:49.620
We're doing a good job.

00:17:49.620 --> 00:17:53.750
And my colleagues and I were saying, yeah,
until we get either a long incubation period

00:17:53.750 --> 00:17:57.940
or people who are asymptomatic or both, and
then you're not going to be ready.

00:17:57.940 --> 00:18:00.690
And they didn't listen to that.

00:18:00.690 --> 00:18:06.830
So I'm very hopeful that in the future we
are able to develop worldwide policies for

00:18:06.830 --> 00:18:11.350
other countries in terms of how things are
done.

00:18:11.350 --> 00:18:18.260
And this is not to, you know, necessarily
be a, you know, critical of other cultures

00:18:18.260 --> 00:18:19.670
for their lifestyles and whatnot.

00:18:19.670 --> 00:18:22.430
No, this is really just a world public health
issue.

00:18:22.430 --> 00:18:25.520
These are just the facts, just facts of bats.

00:18:25.520 --> 00:18:29.880
And we know bats contain a lot of viruses
and they do jump to other species, which then

00:18:29.880 --> 00:18:31.480
can jump to humans.

00:18:31.480 --> 00:18:36.120
We need to take the science and we need to
utilize that as best as we can to make sure

00:18:36.120 --> 00:18:37.500
this doesn't happen again.

00:18:37.500 --> 00:18:43.040
Or if it does, we've got measures in place
that will will stop it very, very quickly

00:18:43.040 --> 00:18:44.530
and not allow this to occur.

00:18:44.530 --> 00:18:49.620
Now, that's hopeful for the future in regards
to your other questions.

00:18:49.620 --> 00:18:55.080
I think early on after people start to get
vaccinated, it's going to take a little while

00:18:55.080 --> 00:19:03.050
for people to just start some anyhow to handshake
or to, you know, congregate in groups or whatnot.

00:19:03.050 --> 00:19:04.120
It's going to be interesting.

00:19:04.120 --> 00:19:06.020
Is it going to make a world of germophobia?

00:19:06.020 --> 00:19:07.760
I hope not.

00:19:07.760 --> 00:19:09.450
I want people to be germ aware.

00:19:09.450 --> 00:19:15.810
We need to be smart about pathogens.

00:19:15.810 --> 00:19:23.610
And and I'm very hopeful that you're you're
in the Boston area where they filmed Jaws.

00:19:23.610 --> 00:19:27.200
You're probably too young to remember this,
but when Jaws came out, there was a huge influx

00:19:27.200 --> 00:19:29.130
of students taking marine biology.

00:19:29.130 --> 00:19:30.130
All right.

00:19:30.130 --> 00:19:31.740
They were majoring in marine biology.

00:19:31.740 --> 00:19:36.510
When in, you know, Indiana Jones came out,
there was a huge influx of students taking

00:19:36.510 --> 00:19:37.820
archeology.

00:19:37.820 --> 00:19:43.250
I'm hoping after this is a huge influx of
people going into microbial ecology, which

00:19:43.250 --> 00:19:45.160
is exactly what we need.

00:19:45.160 --> 00:19:50.830
We need people to understand the interconnectedness
of the very tiny pathogens to the rest of

00:19:50.830 --> 00:19:51.830
the world.

00:19:51.830 --> 00:19:55.220
It's really an arms race between us and pathogens.

00:19:55.220 --> 00:19:58.730
And this was a virus, a relatively weak virus.

00:19:58.730 --> 00:20:03.010
You know, this was I'm not gonna say easy,
but it could have been much worse.

00:20:03.010 --> 00:20:04.010
Oh, my goodness.

00:20:04.010 --> 00:20:07.690
This could have been, you know, imagine if
this were like an Ebola, right?

00:20:07.690 --> 00:20:09.330
It would be horrific.

00:20:09.330 --> 00:20:11.679
It would be a black death time.

00:20:11.679 --> 00:20:15.420
It would be not a good time for this planet.

00:20:15.420 --> 00:20:16.810
The book is.

00:20:16.810 --> 00:20:22.200
So you think you can think tools for having
intelligent conversations and getting along?

00:20:22.200 --> 00:20:26.150
We've been speaking with the book's author,
Dr Christopher DeCarlo.

00:20:26.150 --> 00:20:27.150
So great to have you on.

00:20:27.150 --> 00:20:29.300
I appreciate your time once again.

00:20:29.300 --> 00:20:29.700
My pleasure.

